<body>
<main>

<header class="masthead">
  <div class="eyebrow">CHEMICAL SPECIATION · E-pH ANALYSIS</div>
  <h1>Fe(III) &amp; Cu(II) in a citrate + chloride pot</h1>
  <p class="subhead">How two competing metals share 5 mM citrate and 0.1 M chloride from pH 2 to 10, and what the Nernst equation makes of both couples.</p>
  <div class="compbar">
    <div><span class="k">Fe (total)</span>1.0 mM</div>
    <div><span class="k">Cu (total)</span>1.0 mM</div>
    <div><span class="k">citrate</span>5.0 mM</div>
    <div><span class="k">chloride</span>0.10 M</div>
    <div><span class="k">T</span>25 °C</div>
    <div><span class="k">I</span>0.1 m</div>
  </div>
</header>

<section>
<h2><span class="sec">§1</span>Method &amp; solver</h2>

<p>At each grid point (pH, E) the solver enforces mass balance on every dissolved element and Nernst equilibrium on the two redox couples. Chloride is held at 0.10 M throughout — the metals take at most ~4 mM of Cl, so the free-Cl approximation is safe to under 4%. The unknowns reduce to a single scalar, [Cit<sup>3&minus;</sup>]<sub>free</sub>, and the citrate mass-balance residual is solved by Brent's method between log[Cit<sup>3&minus;</sup>] = &minus;25 and &minus;0.5.</p>

<div class="formula">
Fe<sub>T</sub> = [Fe<sup>3+</sup>] &middot; &alpha;<sub>Fe(III)</sub> + [Fe<sup>2+</sup>] &middot; &alpha;<sub>Fe(II)</sub><br>
Cu<sub>T</sub> = [Cu<sup>2+</sup>] &middot; &alpha;<sub>Cu(II)</sub> + [Cu<sup>+</sup>] &middot; &alpha;<sub>Cu(I)</sub><br>
[Fe<sup>3+</sup>] / [Fe<sup>2+</sup>] = 10<sup>(E &minus; 0.771) / 0.05916</sup><br>
[Cu<sup>2+</sup>] / [Cu<sup>+</sup>]  = 10<sup>(E &minus; 0.153) / 0.05916</sup>
</div>

<p>Each &alpha; is the side-reaction coefficient — the sum over hydrolysis, chloro-, and citrato- complexes weighted by the current [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>], [Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>], [Cit<sup>3&minus;</sup>], and [HCit<sup>2&minus;</sup>]. After the aqueous solve, three solids are tested for saturation and, if oversaturated, cap the corresponding free-ion activity: 2-line ferrihydrite (Fe(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s)), amorphous Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub>(s), and nantokite (CuCl(s)). Cu(s) forms when [Cu<sup>+</sup>] exceeds 10<sup>(E &minus; 0.521)/0.05916</sup>.</p>

<p>Every equilibrium constant used is tabulated in §2. The database follows the critical compilations (NIST 46, Baes &amp; Mesmer's <em>Hydrolysis of Cations</em>, and Martell &amp; Smith's <em>Critical Stability Constants</em>); polynuclear species that are minor at 1 mM metal and I = 0.1 M (the Fe<sub>2</sub>(OH)<sub>2</sub><sup>4+</sup> dimer and the various Cu&ndash;Cit dinuclear pairs) are omitted.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2><span class="sec">§2</span>The receipt: log K, log &beta;, log K<sub>s</sub>, E&deg;</h2>

<p>Every reaction the solver actually evaluated, with its constant and provenance. Signs follow the convention printed in the reaction column.</p>

<table>
<thead>
<tr>
  <th style="width:44%">Reaction</th>
  <th style="width:14%" class="n">log K / log &beta; / log *K</th>
  <th>Source (25 &deg;C)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="grp"><td colspan="3">Citrate acid&ndash;base (I = 0.1 M)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rxn">H<sub>3</sub>L &rlarr; H<sub>2</sub>L<sup>&minus;</sup> + H<sup>+</sup></td><td class="n">pK<sub>a1</sub> = 3.13</td><td>Martell &amp; Smith Vol 3</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rxn">H<sub>2</sub>L<sup>&minus;</sup> &rlarr; HL<sup>2&minus;</sup> + H<sup>+</sup></td><td class="n">pK<sub>a2</sub> = 4.76</td><td>Martell &amp; Smith Vol 3</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rxn">HL<sup>2&minus;</sup> &rlarr; L<sup>3&minus;</sup> + H<sup>+</sup></td><td class="n">pK<sub>a3</sub> = 6.40</td><td>Martell &amp; Smith Vol 3</td></tr>

<tr class="grp"><td colspan="3">Fe(III) hydrolysis (log *&beta;<sub>n</sub>: Fe<sup>3+</sup> + n H<sub>2</sub>O &rlarr; Fe(OH)<sub>n</sub><sup>3&minus;n</sup> + n H<sup>+</sup>)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rxn">Fe<sup>3+</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rlarr; FeOH<sup>2+</sup> + H<sup>+</sup></td><td class="n">&minus;2.19</td><td>Baes &amp; Mesmer 1976</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rxn">Fe<sup>3+</sup> + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O &rlarr; Fe(OH)<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> + 2 H<sup>+</sup></td><td class="n">&minus;5.67</td><td>Baes &amp; Mesmer 1976</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rxn">Fe<sup>3+</sup> + 3 H<sub>2</sub>O &rlarr; Fe(OH)<sub>3</sub>(aq) + 3 H<sup>+</sup></td><td class="n">&minus;13.6</td><td>Baes &amp; Mesmer 1976</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rxn">Fe<sup>3+</sup> + 4 H<sub>2</sub>O &rlarr; Fe(OH)<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> + 4 H<sup>+</sup></td><td class="n">&minus;21.6</td><td>Baes &amp; Mesmer 1976</td></tr>
