<section>
<h2><span class="sec">§3</span>The joint E&ndash;pH picture</h2>

<p>Two Pourbaix maps, one per metal, drawn on the <em>same</em> pot: identical chloride, identical citrate, identical solved [Cit<sup>3&minus;</sup>] at every grid point. Whatever citrate one metal grabs, the other doesn't get. Dashed lines are the H<sub>2</sub>O stability window.</p>

<div class="figrow">
<figure>
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{pourbaix_Fe}" alt="Fe Pourbaix diagram">
<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Dominant Fe species. Below pH ~5.5 the ferric fate is decided between free Fe<sup>2+</sup> (reducing) and the Fe(III)&ndash;citrate complexes (oxidising); the FeCl<sub>n</sub> region never wins &mdash; Fe(III)&ndash;citrate beats Fe(III)&ndash;chloride by ~10 orders of magnitude. Above pH ~5.5, ferrihydrite precipitates and the whole upper half is red-brown.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure>
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{pourbaix_Cu}" alt="Cu Pourbaix diagram">
<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Dominant Cu species. Chloride stabilises Cu(I) so strongly (log &beta;<sub>2</sub><sup>Cu(I)</sup> = 5.5) that CuCl<sub>2</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> owns a big wedge above the Cu(s) line. Cu(II)&ndash;citrate rules the oxic middle-pH belt. Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub>(s) closes the diagram at pH &gt; ~8.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>

<div class="callout">
<p><b>Why the two metals partition so differently.</b> Both Fe(III) and Cu(II) form Cit&ndash;M complexes stronger than either forms with Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>. But Fe(III) is so hard a Lewis acid that ferrihydrite outcompetes citrate above pH 5.5, whereas Cu(II) &mdash; a softer, borderline acid &mdash; keeps its citrate complex intact up to pH 8 before Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub>(s) starts to matter. Chloride only matters for the <em>reduced</em> forms: it hardly touches Fe(II), but it dominates Cu(I) speciation entirely.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section>
<h2><span class="sec">§4</span>Species distributions at fixed E</h2>

<h3>Oxic (E = +0.60 V vs SHE)</h3>
<p>An air-equilibrated water sits near this potential once biology or organic matter has bled off a bit of the O<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O headroom. Under these conditions Fe is trapped as Fe(III), Cu as Cu(II) &mdash; the two metals compete for the same citrate pool.</p>

<div class="figrow">
<figure>{fe_oxic}
<figcaption><b>Figure 3a.</b> Fe(III) is held soluble as FeHCit<sup>+</sup> below pH ~5.5, then ferrihydrite forms sharply and Fe drops out of solution. The bulge of Fe(III)&ndash;citrate you see extends to lower pH because HCit<sup>2&minus;</sup> (not L<sup>3&minus;</sup>) is the operative donor there.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure>{cu_oxic}
<figcaption><b>Figure 3b.</b> Cu is dominated by CuCl<sup>+</sup>/Cu<sup>2+</sup> only at pH &lt; 4; from pH 4&ndash;8 the citrate complexes take over (CuCit<sup>&minus;</sup> then CuCit<sub>2</sub><sup>4&minus;</sup> as HL<sup>2&minus;</sup> deprotonates). Above pH ~8 Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub>(s) precipitates.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>

<h3>Mid (E = +0.15 V vs SHE)</h3>
<p>This slice cuts right through the Fe(III)/Fe(II) crossover at circum-neutral pH and lies just below the Cu(I)/Cu(II) crossover. The two metals behave differently: Fe still ends up as ferrihydrite above pH 6 because Fe(III) is thermodynamically favoured there; Cu is stabilised as Cu(I)&ndash;chloride until citrate can bind Cu(II) strongly enough (~pH 6) to pull the couple over.</p>

<div class="figrow">
<figure>{fe_mid}
<figcaption><b>Figure 4a.</b> At +0.15 V the Fe(III)/Fe(II) couple sits below E&deg;, so free Fe<sup>2+</sup> (or Fe(II)&ndash;citrate) is favoured below pH ~6. Above pH 6, ferrihydrite still wins because Fe(III) is <em>stabilised</em> by hydrolysis, which shifts the couple far positive.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure>{cu_mid}
<figcaption><b>Figure 4b.</b> CuCl<sub>2</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> dominates from pH 2&ndash;6: the huge chloride stabilisation of Cu(I) (log &beta;<sub>2</sub> = 5.5) pushes the effective Cu(II)/Cu(I) potential from +0.153 V down to ~+0.14 V (at 0.1 M Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>). Rising [L<sup>3&minus;</sup>] eventually pulls Cu back to Cu(II) as citrate.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</section>

<section>
<h2><span class="sec">§5</span>Citrate partitioning: who is winning?</h2>

<p>Both figures below show the same 5 mM citrate pool split into three buckets: bound to Fe (rust), bound to Cu (blue), and free/protonated (grey). The ceiling on either metal is stoichiometric: 1 mM of Fe can hold at most 20% of a 5 mM citrate pool at 1:1, or 40% at 1:2.</p>

<div class="figrow">
<figure>{cit_oxic}
<figcaption><b>Figure 5a.</b> Oxic (E = +0.60 V). Fe(III) claims exactly its 1:1 stoichiometric share (20%) from pH 2&ndash;5, then loses it all to ferrihydrite. Cu(II) ramps up over pH 3&ndash;7 and briefly claims ~30% (some of it as CuCit<sub>2</sub><sup>4&minus;</sup>), then loses it to Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub>(s) above pH 8. Above pH 9 essentially all 5 mM citrate is free.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure>{cit_mid}
<figcaption><b>Figure 5b.</b> Mid (E = +0.15 V). Fe(II)&ndash;citrate holds ~20% until ferrihydrite forms; Cu(II)&ndash;citrate is compressed into a narrow pH 6&ndash;8 window because Cu(I) is dominant below that and Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub>(s) forms above.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>

