<section>
<h2><span class="sec">§7</span>Reading the diagram: what actually wins where</h2>

<p><b>pH 2&ndash;3, oxic.</b> Fe(III) is quantitatively FeHCit<sup>+</sup> &mdash; the reaction Fe<sup>3+</sup> + HCit<sup>2&minus;</sup> &rlarr; FeHCit<sup>+</sup> with log K = 12.3 easily beats the strongest FeCl<sub>n</sub> combination (log &beta;<sub>2</sub><sup>Fe(III)</sup> &middot; [Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>]<sup>2</sup> = 2.13 &minus; 2 = 0.13 in log units) at 5 mM citrate. Cu(II) is mostly free Cu<sup>2+</sup> with ~20% as CuCl<sup>+</sup>; citrate is too protonated to compete.</p>

<p><b>pH 4&ndash;5.</b> Citrate has slid past its second pK<sub>a</sub>; CuCit<sup>&minus;</sup> becomes dominant (log K = 5.90 acting on the growing pool of L<sup>3&minus;</sup>). Fe(III) is still on FeHCit<sup>+</sup>. This is the only pH range where both metals actively share the citrate: Fe holds 20%, Cu takes another 20% &mdash; and 60% of the citrate is still free/protonated. There is no thermodynamic competition here: 5 mM citrate is plenty for 2 mM total metal, even at a 1:1 stoichiometry.</p>

<p><b>pH 5.5&ndash;6.</b> Ferrihydrite crashes out at these citrate levels &mdash; the mixed FeCit(OH)<sup>&minus;</sup> pathway is not strong enough to keep pace with Fe(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s). Fe abandons the citrate pool entirely. Cu doubles down: with FeCit<sup>+</sup> gone, [L<sup>3&minus;</sup>] rises, and Cu shifts from CuCit<sup>&minus;</sup> toward CuCit<sub>2</sub><sup>4&minus;</sup> (log &beta;<sub>2</sub> = 8.50).</p>

<p><b>pH 7&ndash;8.</b> Cu is roughly half CuCit<sup>&minus;</sup>, half CuCit<sub>2</sub><sup>4&minus;</sup>. Then near pH 8 the Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub>(s) line crosses and copper follows iron into the solid. The freed citrate simply stays as L<sup>3&minus;</sup>.</p>

<p><b>pH 9&ndash;10.</b> Both metals are essentially quantitative solids. Any residual soluble Fe is Fe(OH)<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> (~10<sup>&minus;9</sup> M), any residual Cu is CuCit<sub>2</sub><sup>4&minus;</sup> at similarly trace levels.</p>

<p><b>Reducing side (E &lt; 0.2 V).</b> Iron drops to Fe(II), where citrate binding is 7 orders of magnitude weaker (log K = 4.4 vs 11.4). Fe(II) still binds citrate above pH 5 because chloride is even weaker, but the driving force is small. Copper's behaviour is entirely different: below the Cu(s) line (~0 to +0.1 V, moving with pH) the metal plates out; between +0.1 and ~+0.4 V, CuCl<sub>2</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> owns the solution because the chloride stabilisation of Cu(I) is enormous. The Cu(II)/Cu(I) effective potential in 0.1 M Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> and 5 mM citrate at pH 5 is roughly:</p>

<div class="formula">
E'  =  0.153  &minus;  0.05916 &middot; log{{ &alpha;<sub>Cu(I)</sub>(0.1 M Cl) / &alpha;<sub>Cu(II)</sub>([L<sup>3&minus;</sup>]) }}<br>
     &approx;  0.153  &minus;  0.05916 &middot; log{{ 10<sup>3.5</sup> / (10<sup>2.5</sup>) }}  &approx;  +0.09 V
</div>

<p>i.e. citrate binding of Cu(II) partly cancels the chloride stabilisation of Cu(I), and the couple ends up an order of magnitude closer to zero than its bare value.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2><span class="sec">§8</span>Caveats</h2>

<ul>
<li><b>Ferrihydrite solubility is soft.</b> log *K<sub>s</sub> = 3.5 is a fresh 2-line ferrihydrite. Aged goethite is 10&ndash;100&times; less soluble, which would push the Fe(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s) boundary down to pH ~4 and eliminate the Fe(III)&ndash;citrate region on the oxic side. If your pot is minutes old, use 3.5; if it has aged for days, use 1.5 and expect the diagram to redraw.</li>
<li><b>Polynuclear Fe(III)&ndash;citrate.</b> At 1 mM Fe and pH 5&ndash;7, Fe<sub>2</sub>Cit<sub>2</sub>(OH)<sub>2</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup> and related trimers actually stabilise Fe(III) in solution well beyond what the mononuclear model predicts. They're omitted here for clarity, but they push the ferrihydrite line up ~1 pH unit if you include them.</li>
<li><b>Cu(II)&ndash;citrate polynuclear.</b> The Cu<sub>2</sub>Cit<sub>2</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup> dimer is real at millimolar Cu but again small; including it broadens the CuCit domain by a few tenths of a pH unit.</li>
<li><b>[Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>] held constant.</b> Total Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> is 100 mM and no metal takes more than ~4 mM, so the free-Cl approximation is at worst 4%.</li>
<li><b>Ionic strength.</b> Constants are cited at I = 0.1 M where possible. Corrections to and from I = 0 use Davies where extrapolation was needed. Systematic error on any log K is ~&pm; 0.2 units.</li>
<li><b>No CO<sub>2</sub>.</b> Real air-equilibrated solutions carry ~10<sup>&minus;5</sup> M carbonate at pH 8, which would compete for Cu (CuCO<sub>3</sub>) and slightly for Fe (siderite is not stable at these E's). Add carbonate explicitly if the pot is open to air.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<footer style="margin-top:56px; padding-top:20px; border-top:1px solid var(--rule); font-size:12px; color:var(--muted); font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;">
Solver: Brent's method on log[Cit<sup>3&minus;</sup>] &isin; [&minus;25, &minus;0.5], 81 &times; 71 grid over pH 2&ndash;10 &times; E &minus;0.4 to +1.0 V &middot; SciPy 1.17
</footer>

</main>
</body>
